cruise ship passengers who unexpectedly  spent nearly two weeks helping hurricane  Irma victims are finally going home  Royal Caribbean sends some of its ships  to the Caribbean to help with the relief  effort one of them the majesty of the  Seas was in the middle of a five-day  cruise when it joined the humanitarian  mission DeMarco Morgan is at the port of  Miami's cruise ship terminal with how  passengers on board volunteered to stay  and help out DeMarco good morning to you  good morning Gail what started out as a  relaxing cruise quickly turned into a  rescue mission Royal Caribbean ended its  five-day cruise one day early and gave  passengers the option of tagging along  for its relief and rescue efforts of  course they are all well riding out the  storm at sea we spent you know 16 days  17 days together on sea together and we  all got to know each other in an  environment and a really tough  environment Robert Mahoney and his wife  were part of a group of around 70  passengers who stayed on board the ship  after the crew announced it their plans  to head to the islands devastated by her  mo this would be an opportunity to help  people out down the road we were glad to  stay on board and help out any way we  could the ship picked up passengers in  Saint Martin and st. Thomas what's  usually a party boat turned into a  floating shelter housing hundreds of  people  some of the rescued sing for joy when  the ship arrived in Puerto Rico and  thank to the crew that came to their  rescue it was horrible  what tango we made it thank you James  good job reception that we're warm  I saw people get off they ship the next  day and they were there almost whole  again within 24 hours  Royal Caribbean chairman and CEO Richard  fain was touched by how many of the  passengers stayed on board they would  talk to them they would help them they  wanted to help us distribute supplies  and so we were actually a little  surprised but quite pleased  sometimes disaster brings out the best  and sometimes the worst here I think we  saw the best in so many people I would  do it again tomorrow anything I could do  to help somebody you know talk to  somebody bring somebody back in the  lowest of all would be my absolute  pleasure now the CEO says the passengers  and crew were never in danger from urban  because the ship was able to hide behind  the mountains of Cuba and wait for the  storm to pass through so they could head  to those affected islands beyond great  story these people went from everyday  vacationers to humanitarians good for  them no regrets about it no regrets they  loved every minute of it DeMarco thank  you
 

